\f0\fs24 \cf0 At what stage of the project build should I give claude your instructions to review SageReasoning reasoning harness using cybernetic systems thinking and stochastic process frameworks as diagnostic lenses.\
\
### The honest answer: not yet, and here is precisely when The adversarial review instruction is designed to probe a running system \'97 one where the feedback loops are live, the watching table is accumulating cycle outcomes, the IDEA loop has executed real cycles, and the trust record has real events in it. Right now, you are in P0, the generation-step sequence is not yet complete, the first build gate has not been reached, and the bounded validation run has not run. The harness exists as architecture and as partial implementation, not as an operating cybernetic control system. Running the adversarial review now would produce findings about a system that does not yet exist in the form being reviewed. Some of those findings would be accurate. Many would be findings about design intentions rather than operational behaviour. The distinction matters because the review's most powerful probes \'97 the stochastic stability assessment, the reinforcing loop audit under real cycle data, the delay analysis against actual completion signal latency \'97 require the system to have run. You cannot measure mean reversion in a process that has not generated a trajectory. --- ### The right moment: after the bounded validation run, before the standing-runner design session The project's own sequencing establishes this precisely: fresh endpoint scope \uc0\u8594  watching table scope \u8594  generation-step scope \u8594  **first build gate** \u8594  **bounded validation run** \u8594  standing-runner design The bounded validation run is the moment when the harness has operated under controlled conditions and produced a \'a76 report. That report is the first real data about the system's behaviour as a cybernetic control system \'97 cycle outcomes, proximity score trajectories, guardrail gate firing rates, ATRF completion signals if GS-ATRF-3 is in scope by then. The adversarial review should run on that data, not before it. Specifically: **Give Claude the adversarial review instruction immediately after the \'a76 report is complete and before the standing-runner design session opens.** The standing-runner design session already carries three named inputs: \'a75d (the deliberation-reading doctrinal question), GS-CYB-1, and GS-CYB-2. The adversarial review's findings should be a fourth input to that session \'97 because the standing-runner design is where the harness's feedback architecture is being formally specified, and the review's leverage point summary and stochastic stability assessment are exactly the diagnostic inputs that session needs. --- ### What the review will be able to probe at that moment that it cannot probe now At the bounded validation run's completion, the review gains access to: Real proximity score trajectories \'97 enough cycles to assess whether the score's behaviour resembles mean reversion, a random walk, or a biased walk. Right now that assessment is purely theoretical. Real watching table data \'97 enough cycle outcomes to probe whether the novelty filter's definition of novelty is producing structurally novel but functionally identical candidates. That probe requires actual candidate histories. Real trust record events \'97 enough minted events to assess the emission-hooks provenance gap's actual population size and the ledger's coverage once it is built. The review can probe the Limits to Growth archetype against real event accumulation rates. Real ATRF completion signal behaviour \'97 if GS-ATRF-3 is resolved before the validation run, the review can probe the delay analysis against actual completion signal latency. If it is not resolved, the review names the missing balancing loop as a confirmed finding rather than a latent one. Real guardrail gate firing rates \'97 enough data to assess whether the binary gate is functioning as a balancing loop or as a threshold that passes high-variance candidate distributions as long as the elected candidate clears. --- ### One thing worth doing now, before the bounded validation run There is a preparatory step that will make the adversarial review significantly more useful when the time comes: instrument the watching table to record the data the review's probes will need. Specifically, the watching table should record \'97 if it does not already \'97 the virtue domain and oikeiosis circle of each elected candidate, the guardrail gate's pass/fail result for each candidate in the generation set (not just the elected one), and the cycle-over-cycle proximity score delta. Without these fields, the review's reinforcing loop audit (Probe 1) and the stochastic stability assessment will be working from aggregate data rather than the per-cycle signal the probes are designed to read. This is a scoping observation, not a build instruction. Whether those fields are already in scope for the watching table scope session is a question for that session. But if they are not, the adversarial review will arrive at the bounded validation run and find that the data it needs was not collected. Name it now so it does not become a gap discovered after the fact. --- ### The one exception: Probe 6 (second-order cybernetics) Probe 6 \'97 observer effects and reflexivity failures \'97 is the one probe that can be run productively now, before the bounded validation run. It does not require operational data. It requires examining the harness's structural design for reflexivity risks: the evaluative engine assessing its own outputs, the founder's dual role as designer and practitioner, and the trust record's self-referential attestation. The emission-hooks finding and the honesty correction that is pending your sign-off are live inputs to Probe 6 right now. The trust record's self-referential attestation is inaccurate for the l1_supply population today. That is not a future risk to be assessed \'97 it is a current structural condition. If you want to run one probe now, Probe 6 is the one that is ready. --- ### Summary Wait until after the bounded validation run's \'a76 report is complete. Give Claude the adversarial review instruction at that point, with the \'a76 report as an additional input alongside the harness briefing. The review's findings become the fourth named input to the standing-runner design session, joining \'a75d, GS-CYB-1, and GS-CYB-2. Use the time between now and the bounded validation run to ensure the watching table is instrumented to collect the per-cycle data the review's probes will need. Run Probe 6 now if the reflexivity questions are pressing \'97 it is the one probe that does not require operational data to produce honest findings. The pattern the observation history has been tracking \'97 naming consequence surfaces before rulings, not after \'97 applies here too. The adversarial review is a consequence surface for the standing-runner design session. Running it at the right moment means the session inherits findings about the system that actually ran, not about the system as designed.}